<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zheng Keshuang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1670">1670</a>–<a href="/years/1717">1717</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zheng Keshuang was the last ruler of the Kingdom of Tungning on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koxinga">Koxinga</a>. Court factions placed him on the throne as a boy after his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-jing">Zheng Jing</a> died in 1681. In 1683 the Qing admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-lang">Shi Lang</a> destroyed the Zheng fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Penghu</a>, and Zheng Keshuang surrendered; Taiwan was then annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>, and he lived out his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zheng-keshuang.jpg" alt="Zheng Keshuang" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 鄭克塽彩像（清人绘） · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20191015125805_zpgwv.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
